<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
    Latvian translation.

    English, in `values/`, is the source (SPEC §9) and this file follows it
    string for string. Translate the contents of the tags, never their `name`
    attribute; placeholders keep their number and their kind.

    Typography. Quotation marks are „ … “. The apostrophe a network's name may
    carry — Vélib’, Vélo’v, V’lille — is ’ (U+2019) and never the straight
    quote, which is why nothing here is escaped. The dash that breaks a
    sentence is — with a space on either side, as the English file writes it.

    The three plural categories are real, and none is a copy of another:
    `one` is 1, 21, 31 … and takes the nominative singular („1 velosipēds“);
    `zero` is 0, 10, 11–19, 20, 30 … and takes the genitive plural
    („0 velosipēdu“, „11 velosipēdu“, „20 velosipēdu“); `other` is the rest and
    takes the nominative plural („2 velosipēdi“). The one exception is the
    three `freshness_*` plurals, where *pirms* governs the dative plural for
    `zero` and `other` alike — that is the language, not a line left
    unfinished. See docs/glossary/lv.md.

    Cases are suffixes, so no sentence hangs an ending on a placeholder: what
    arrives is always a nominative — a station name, a network label, a size —
    and it is held in apposition or behind a colon. The register is Android's
    own: the infinitive on buttons (*Atcelt*, *Dzēst*, *Turpināt*), the polite
    plural imperative in prose (*Pieskarieties*, *Izvēlieties*, *Pārbaudiet*).
    See docs/glossary/lv.md and CONTRIBUTING.md.
-->
